# Set the default behavior, in case people don't have core.autocrlf set.
* text=auto

# Declare files that will always have CRLF line endings on checkout.
*.patch text eol=crlf

# Shell scripts must always have LF endings — they are executed on Linux
# (container entrypoints, Lambda helpers, CI scripts). CRLF endings cause
# the kernel to interpret the shebang as `/bin/bash\r`, which fails with
# "no such file or directory" at exec time.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The web entry point must always have LF endings, because its inline <script>
# blocks are allowed by the Content-Security-Policy through SHA-256 hashes of
# their exact bytes (infra/lib/helper/cspInlineScriptHashes.ts). A hash covers
# every byte between the tags, so the same script hashes differently under CRLF
# than under LF: a checkout whose endings differ from the recorded form serves a
# page whose inline scripts the browser silently refuses to run, and nothing
# fails at build or deploy time. Pinning the file removes the platform from the
# equation.
#
# An existing clone keeps whatever endings its working tree already has, because
# the attribute is applied on checkout. `git add --renormalize` does NOT fix it —
# that rewrites the index, and the index here is already LF. Nor does `git
# checkout` on its own: git skips writing a file whose cached stat still matches
# the index, which is exactly the state a pre-attribute clone is in. The file has
# to be removed first, from the repository root:
#
#     rm web/index.html && git checkout -- web/index.html
#     (cmd.exe: del web\index.html, then git checkout -- web/index.html)
#
# infra/test/cspInlineScriptHashes.test.ts fails until this is done, which is the
# intended warning: a build from a CRLF checkout serves scripts the browser
# refuses to run.
web/index.html text eol=lf